Thanks to all who responded via e-mail.  My problem is solved.  Here are the
responses:

I believe you set the wrong flag when you created your "key" file.  In
your <subsetname>.k file, in the section for subset definitions, is the
third field something other than 2?  If so, you've just set your subset
to be non-delete-able, hence the error you received.  Try changing it to
"2", and all should be well.

Sue Gleeson


========

I suspect a couple of problems.

Check your .k file.  The line that defines the BFVSRV109 subset should look
something like:

        BFVSRV109    .    2    'BARCO FlexiVision Server Dist.'

The third field (a 2 here) is propogated to the .ctrl file in the form:

        FLAGS=2

If FLAGS is an odd number, you will be unable to delete the subset.

Another cause can be if the .scp file denies deletion.  If I recall
correctly,
the .scp
file should have a case statement based on the ACTion being requested when
the
.scp
is invoked.  The cases that are relevant here are:  PRE_D (for code to be
executed
prior to subset deletion) and POST_D (code to be executed after the subset
is
deleted).

Deletion is denied if the PRE_D phase exits with a value other than 0.

If neither of these issues is the cause of your problem, you could e-mail me
a
copy of
your .ctrl file and a copy of the .scp file for this subset, I will see if
I can quickly spot
the problem.

I don't claim to be the setld expert, but I have been building kits for the
past couple
of years and have some experience with these tools.

-Derek Haining
 ==================

Very good information from all.  I was using a script to create my .k file.
I had a "place-holder" for the flags value in a text file and used sed to
filter in the my desired value.  (This was done because I have more than one
subset in the kit). Anyway, I had sed pass a "0" in, but what came out was
"01" in the .k file.  So to fix the problem and make my life more simpler, I
am passing "2" in as the flags value for all subsets. (per the suggestions
here).  It now works fine.  I didn't know it had to be an even number
though.  That's interesting.

Thanks again,

Keith




