Frank J. Perricone <frank@dlc.state.vt.us> wrote:

: <rsimpson@ewrcsdra.CutThisOut.demon.co.uk> wrote in message
:> : They're also laughably understaffed; just four people, most of them
:> : part-time only, handling a project that's spanning millions of
: computers.
:> I don't buy this "They haven't made the code available because they
:> don't have time" argument.
: Good thing I never said anything remotely like it, then.  They have much
: better reasons not to make the code available, such as:

Hmmmm, I can't quite see the difference between "laughably understaffed"
(your words) and "don't have time" (my words).  So far as I can see, they
boil down to exactly the same thing.

: * scientific rigor requiring exactly the same processing on every platform

MY GOD THIS IS PAINFULL!!!  Let me spell it out as simply as I possibly can
for those who still can't grasp it!!!  The above explanation is bollocks for
2 simple reasons.

1) As I write http://setiathome.ssl.berkeley.edu/stats/platforms.html shows
that 155212 work units have been received from optimised Tru64 clients.  S@H
would never have accepted those WUs if they didn't genuinly believe that the
Tru64 clients do exactly the same processing as all the other clients. 
Would they?  They can easily block results from clients they don't like. 
There is no evidence that they are stupid.  Think about it!!!!

2) Plenty of other platforms have produced optimised clients and everyone
seems perfectly happy. For example, mips and mips4 or i386-pc-linux and
i686-pc-linux. 

: * no need to optimize the clients because they can't keep up with the ones
: they have as it is

Ahh, now, I have absolutely no problem at all with believing that this is
the real reason.  Unfortunately, it simply raises the question "If S@H
aren't interested in optimised clients why did the give the source code to
Compaq?".

: * keeping control of their own project

No, sorry, don't understand this.  If S@H receive a new client, test it to
ensure that it does the correct processing (which, as I have proved above,
must have been done) and then release it to the user community they still
keep control of their own project.  After all they have already done this on
several previous occasions.

: However, that's not what we're talking about -- we're talking about why they
: didn't update their website.

Ahhh, well you may be talking about why they didn't update their website,
but I'm talking about why they haven't made the Tru64 clients available.  As
I have already pointed out (possibly in another thread) these two topics are
not necessarily linked.  They could just bung it on their FTP site and let
Compaq tell folks where it is, or they could just say to Compaq "Sorry, were
a bit busy, can you distribute the client until we get a chance to sort our
web site out".

Also, since this thread started S@H have updated the Text Clients download
page (apparently on Sep 13th) and the Tru64 clients are still not there. 
That suggests to me that web page updating is not the problem.

: One would assume people in these newsgroups have had experience
: with system crashes; it'd be nice to imagine a little bit of courtesy and
: compassion on the part of those who've been through similar things.

Nope, sorry.  This is just the "they're understaffed / too busy" argument
under a different guise.  Over the last couple of months there have been
crashes at S@H, but there have been plenty of days where it was all working
fine.

: Far too
: many people are forgetting that *we* are supposed to be helping *them* --
: that this is about science, not glory.

Well, some are in it for the science and some for the glory.  I don't mind
people considering my motives shallow.


