In article <37E705E1.D1303F3A@cwo.com.au>, Wallace Liu
<wallace.liu@cwo.com.au> wrote:
>I am confused by the output of printf command:
>
>d=07
>printf "%02d\n"  $d
>the system print 07;
>
>d=05
>printf "%02d\n"  $d
>the system print 05;
>
>d=09
>printf "%02d\n"  $d
>the system print this message to me:
>printf: 09 not completely converted
>00
>
>d=08
>printf "%02d\n"  $d
>the system print this message to me:
>printf: 08 not completely converted
>00
>
>I come cross this problem on Solaris 2.6 and Compaq UNIX. Why printf command
>does not complete convert 09 and 08?



I am making several assumptions

1) this is peril

2) that the d=08 and d=09 are initially treated as strings.

3) that the printf is converting the strings to numbers.

It is my guess that when the strings are being converted, the standard
convention, as established in the early days of UNIX and 'C' (maybe
even earlier), was that any number beginning with a leading zero was
treated as an octal number, and octal numbers only use digits 0-7.  The
digits 8 and 9 are illegal in octal numbers.

If you specify the values as:

        d=8
        d=9

then I think the printf statement would work correctly.



