Michael Kraemer (kraemer@clri6f.gsi.de) wrote:

: The other direction (UNIX -> ANSI tape) will give you more trouble,
: because an ordinary UNIX has no concept of "records" as opposed to 
: proprietary OSes like VMS or IBMs MVS. 

dd on Tu64 and some other UNIX systems will convert to/from fixed length
text records (a common format on tape) and CR separated byte stream records
(a la UNIX).  Sometimes this is helpfull.  See the block and unblock options 
on dd's manpage.

I often end up repeating these steps since not all UNIX support ANSI standard
tape labeling:

1 dd if=/dev/nrt0 of=/dev/tty
2 dd conv=unblock if=/dev/nrt0 of=filename bs=blocksize cbs=recordsize
3 dd if=/dev/nrt0 of=/dev/tty

Step 1 copies the ANSI header to the terminal, you can read the filename 
from the header, record type, block size, and record size.  Step 2 copies 
the data to a disk file, substitute the correct file name, record size, 
and conversion options.  Step 3 copies the file trailer to the terminal 
so you  can use step 1 on the next file's label.

You must use the no-rewind device (/dev/n-something) until you've copied the 
last file.  If step 1 produces no output, that's the logical end of tape.

But of course, there are many conversions dd can't do for you.

