Frank J. Perricone <frank@dlc.state.vt.us> wrote:
: (BTW, the SETI@home team probably hasn't updated their website because
: they've been having problems the last week or two with server crashes.
: They're also laughably understaffed; just four people, most of them
: part-time only, handling a project that's spanning millions of computers.
: They need more than that just to handle the press they're getting!)

I don't buy this "They haven't made the code available because they
don't have time" argument.  If they don't have time to update the web
page they could do either of the following in no time at all.

1) Copy the client to their ftp site and send a brief email to Compaq
asking them to publicise its location.

Still too difficult?

2) Just send a brief email to Compaq asking them to distribute it until
S@H have time to get a grip on their web site.

Finally, how come they are so short of help?  Aren't they at a University?
Don't they have thousands of students who would just love to be closely
involved with a project like this?  Don't they have lecturers coming
along and saying "Each of our students needs to do a project as part of
their course.  Do you have any ideas?"?

When I was a 1st year student there was a very small and overworked
research group in the department who were builing a satellite.  I went
along and volunteered to help.  They gave me a few mundane jobs to do
and when they discovered that I was keen and competent they got me
to build a small part of the satellite.  Let me tell you that if you are
a 1st year electronics student and get some of your soldering into orbit
it means a lot!  I think I was the only undergraduate, but there were
several postgrad student involved.  I worked every hour that God sent
and my exams suffered, but it was worth it.  If a graduate came to
me now with a relatively poor degree, but could demonstrate that he or
she had done something like that with their spare time I would give them
a job!

Perhaps modern Californian youth are far too busy drinking and surfing
to be bothered?

