>In article <7rio1r$fgq$1@news.is.co.za>, jasperh <jasperh@debis.co.za> wrote:
>> I have installed a 9.1GB Quantun AtlasIV Disk in my AlphaStation 200 4/100
>> (running DecUnix 3.2c), but it seems to me that only 7.8GB is available to
>> the system. Is this normal for this type disk on this system??

>[...]
>  -m minfree
>	    Specifies the percentage of	space held back	from normal users;
>	    the	minimum	free space threshold.  The default value used is 10%.
>	    This value can be set to zero, however up to a factor of three in
>	    throughput will be lost over the performance obtained at a 10%
>	    threshold.	Note that if the value is raised above the current
>	    usage level, users will be unable to allocate files	until enough
>	    files have been deleted to get under the higher threshold.

The 10% reservation in UFS (btw, root can use it) is a remarkably simple
and effective technique to make fragmentation not be a problem.

On a real-life timesharing system, a flock of users that fills up your
new disk will fill it up even if you cut minfree back.

10% is leaves remarkably little fragmentation, cutting back to 5% may be
okay, but when the disk is full, UFS will search twice as far to find the
next chunk of available storage.  (A gross simplification, but a handy
rule-of-thumb.)

This is true of UFS in general, not Tru64 in particular.
